WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.440 --> 00:00:05.430
It's not often that an actor gets to say goodbye
to a character on his own terms, especially

00:00:05.430 --> 00:00:08.719
when it comes to big-budget superhero action-fests.

00:00:08.719 --> 00:00:13.499
More often than not, an actor's movie flops,
or he decides not to come back for the sequel,

00:00:13.499 --> 00:00:18.460
or merciless producers kill characters off
to make room for more exciting heroes.

00:00:18.460 --> 00:00:23.970
But in the case of Wolverine, Hugh Jackman
got the chance to go out with a bang in 2017's

00:00:23.970 --> 00:00:24.970
Logan.

00:00:24.970 --> 00:00:28.500
And while the ending was pretty straightforward
in a lot of ways, there are definitely some

00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:33.329
lingering questions both within the story
and in the real world that are worth discussing.

00:00:33.329 --> 00:00:38.040
Pop your claws, light a cigar, and watch out
for spoilers while we test out some theories

00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:41.129
to try to explain the ending of Logan.

00:00:41.129 --> 00:00:43.179
What was across the border?

00:00:43.179 --> 00:00:47.769
Throughout the film, Logan embarked on a quest
to deliver Laura to Eden, someplace in North

00:00:47.769 --> 00:00:52.929
Dakota that would provide sanctuary for her
and every other mutant kid who escaped the

00:00:52.929 --> 00:00:56.179
Transigen program run by Doctor Rice.

00:00:56.179 --> 00:01:00.319
Logan's skeptical because he thinks it came
out of a comic book, only to discover that

00:01:00.319 --> 00:01:04.500
Eden was real, and that the kids were going
to trek a few miles through the woods to make

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:06.590
it over the Canadian border to safety.

00:01:06.590 --> 00:01:10.300
Only, we never quite find out just what was
across that border.

00:01:10.300 --> 00:01:14.690
Obviously something was waiting for them up
there, and it was powerful and important enough

00:01:14.690 --> 00:01:19.290
that Rice, Pierce, and the Reavers had to
nab the kids before they crossed the border.

00:01:19.290 --> 00:01:25.020
If you're a longtime X-Men fan, you may already
know what...or who...we're obliquely referring

00:01:25.020 --> 00:01:26.020
to.

00:01:26.020 --> 00:01:28.660
Canada, powerful, mutants...

00:01:28.660 --> 00:01:30.140
Alpha Flight

00:01:30.140 --> 00:01:33.170
That's right, it's Canada's premiere super-team
of mutants.

00:01:33.170 --> 00:01:37.510
Alpha Flight was sort of a combination of
the X-Men and the Avengers, but covered in

00:01:37.510 --> 00:01:40.480
maple syrup and always carrying hockey sticks.

00:01:40.480 --> 00:01:45.501
In short, there's plenty of reason to believe
that some cinematic adaptation of Alpha Flight

00:01:45.501 --> 00:01:49.290
was waiting to receive and rescue the kids
across the northern border.

00:01:49.290 --> 00:01:53.280
And because Alpha Flight has usually been
depicted as working as a part the Canadian

00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:57.290
government, it makes sense that they wouldn't
legally be allowed to cross into American

00:01:57.290 --> 00:02:00.659
territory to rescue the X-23 kids directly.

00:02:00.659 --> 00:02:04.830
But once they're in Canadian jurisdiction,
they'd be able to blast any Reaver stupid

00:02:04.830 --> 00:02:05.950
enough to follow.

00:02:05.950 --> 00:02:09.590
And there's plenty of real-world evidence
that Alpha Flight was waiting under the shade

00:02:09.590 --> 00:02:11.890
of some pine trees for the kids.

00:02:11.890 --> 00:02:16.771
For starters, 20th Century Fox has still only
barely scratched the surface in terms of the

00:02:16.771 --> 00:02:20.140
characters it can use to build its comic book
cinematic universe.

00:02:20.140 --> 00:02:24.640
Fox has the movie rights to pretty much every
character associated with the X-Men.

00:02:24.640 --> 00:02:29.480
That clearly includes Alpha Flight, which
debuted as a partial explanation of Wolverine's

00:02:29.480 --> 00:02:34.530
then-mysterious backstory in X-Men #120, back
in 1979.

00:02:34.530 --> 00:02:39.730
To this point, the movie franchise has made
only very oblique references to Alpha Flight,

00:02:39.730 --> 00:02:43.950
most notably in an on-screen easter egg in
X2: X-Men United.

00:02:43.950 --> 00:02:49.920
But in February 2017, X-Men producer Simon
Kinberg told Comicbook.com that there are

00:02:49.920 --> 00:02:52.750
potential plans to develop an "X-Flight" movie.

00:02:52.750 --> 00:02:56.860
That's likely either a slip of the tongue,
and that he meant to say "Alpha Flight," or

00:02:56.860 --> 00:03:01.310
the franchise is being changed to brand it
closer to the rest of the films in the franchise.

00:03:01.310 --> 00:03:05.360
But either way, don't be surprised to hear
that the follow-up to Logan is the start of

00:03:05.360 --> 00:03:07.520
an Alpha Flight-related franchise.

00:03:07.520 --> 00:03:08.970
Unless...

00:03:08.970 --> 00:03:10.650
Generation X?

00:03:10.650 --> 00:03:14.510
We don't know anything about the woman on
the other end of the radio during Rictor's

00:03:14.510 --> 00:03:17.780
chat with whoever was in Canada, waiting for
the kids.

00:03:17.780 --> 00:03:22.090
Maybe it was one of the members of Alpha Flight
— or maybe it was another famous X-Men character

00:03:22.090 --> 00:03:25.870
who's long been associated with Wolverine:
Jubilee.

00:03:25.870 --> 00:03:30.599
In the '90s, the Generation X series was a
more modern take on the New Mutants team that

00:03:30.599 --> 00:03:32.460
had morphed into X-Force by that point.

00:03:32.460 --> 00:03:37.450
Jubilee, who at times has been kind of an
unofficial sidekick for Wolverine in the comics,

00:03:37.450 --> 00:03:41.950
was a main component of the team, as was the
White Queen, Emma Frost.

00:03:41.950 --> 00:03:46.430
Both characters have been established in the
X-Men films in different ways, and the franchise's

00:03:46.430 --> 00:03:50.320
murky timelines make it entirely possible
that the woman on the radio could've been

00:03:50.320 --> 00:03:51.349
either one.

00:03:51.349 --> 00:03:55.190
While this isn't quite as fun as the Alpha
Flight theory, it's just as possible that

00:03:55.190 --> 00:03:59.879
the end of Logan could be setting up a possible
Generation X film, with a whole batch of young

00:03:59.879 --> 00:04:03.489
mutants to learn under the instruction of
these veteran characters.

00:04:03.489 --> 00:04:04.800
The eulogy

00:04:04.800 --> 00:04:09.930
After Laura buries Logan in the woods of North
Dakota, she delivers a eulogy that, while

00:04:09.930 --> 00:04:12.680
meaningful, might have left some viewers confused.

00:04:12.680 --> 00:04:17.600
Don't feel bad — it was easy to get distracted
by the delightful mayhem throughout the movie,

00:04:17.600 --> 00:04:19.020
so you may have missed this.

00:04:19.020 --> 00:04:23.150
Laura was quoting the closing monologue from
Shane, which she watched in the hotel room

00:04:23.150 --> 00:04:24.150
with Xavier.

00:04:24.150 --> 00:04:27.560
"There's no living with... with a killing.

00:04:27.560 --> 00:04:30.430
There's no going back from one.

00:04:30.430 --> 00:04:33.020
Right or wrong, it's a brand.

00:04:33.120 --> 00:04:34.120
A brand sticks."

00:04:34.580 --> 00:04:38.380
And it wasn't just a random reference, of
course: Shane was a western about a gunslinger

00:04:38.380 --> 00:04:41.080
putting his spurs on again for one final fight.

00:04:41.080 --> 00:04:42.080
Sound familiar?

00:04:42.080 --> 00:04:47.370
"Now you run on home to your mother, and tell
her... tell her everything's all right.

00:04:48.260 --> 00:04:51.400
And there aren't any more guns in the valley."

00:04:51.410 --> 00:04:52.690
X Marks the spot

00:04:52.690 --> 00:04:57.000
After all the kids have left, Laura picks
up the little wooden cross that marked Logan's

00:04:57.000 --> 00:04:59.930
grave and tilts it on its side, making an
X.

00:04:59.930 --> 00:05:03.060
That's a pretty obvious nod to, well, the
X-Men.

00:05:03.060 --> 00:05:07.660
But it's also a pretty beautiful, quiet statement
about the kind of guy Wolverine was throughout

00:05:07.660 --> 00:05:08.660
all these movies.

00:05:08.660 --> 00:05:12.970
The cross at a gravesite is meant to reflect
a person's faith in God, in something higher

00:05:12.970 --> 00:05:14.000
than them.

00:05:14.000 --> 00:05:15.860
Obviously Logan wasn't much of a Christian...

00:05:23.300 --> 00:05:27.880
...but he was an X-Man, and he spent his life
fighting for the betterment of mutantkind

00:05:27.880 --> 00:05:29.270
and the ideals of Charles Xavier.

00:05:29.270 --> 00:05:34.210
The fact that he devoted the final years of
his life to taking care of Xavier shows that,

00:05:34.210 --> 00:05:38.620
despite his insistence that he didn't care
about much, he believed in Xavier and his

00:05:38.620 --> 00:05:42.060
dream of peace and prosperity more than he
might have admitted.

00:05:42.060 --> 00:05:43.060
Resurrection?

00:05:43.060 --> 00:05:45.730
Now, you can't keep a good hero down.

00:05:45.730 --> 00:05:50.210
Hugh Jackman has clearly said goodbye to the
role that made him famous with Logan, so chances

00:05:50.210 --> 00:05:52.390
are pretty slim that he'll put the claws on
again.

00:05:52.390 --> 00:05:53.890
But this is Wolverine.

00:05:53.890 --> 00:05:59.600
He's on par with Batman, Superman, and Spider-Man
in terms of recognizability and popularity.

00:05:59.600 --> 00:06:03.960
He's an incredibly valuable brand to Fox,
and you'd be crazy if you thought a little

00:06:03.960 --> 00:06:08.850
thing like Hugh Jackman's retirement might
stop them from putting Wolverine in more movies.

00:06:08.850 --> 00:06:13.100
This is definitely not the last time we'll
see Wolverine on film — and frankly, don't

00:06:13.100 --> 00:06:15.790
even believe that Jackman's as done as he
says he is.

00:06:15.790 --> 00:06:16.900
They always come back, man.

00:06:16.900 --> 00:06:21.770
If an old dude like Spock can come back to
life, Wolverine — a super mutant with a

00:06:21.770 --> 00:06:25.040
super healing factor—certainly can.

00:06:27.920 --> 00:06:29.000
Thanks for watching!

